1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,465
Your app talks to a server you'll never
see.

2
00:00:02,705 --> 00:00:07,051
What exactly is it saying? And why do you
never need to know how that server

3
00:00:07,051 --> 00:00:07,394
works?

4
00:00:07,634 --> 00:00:12,672
That's an API. By the end of this video,
you'll read one, run one, and spot the

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:14,266
mistakes that break them.

6
00:00:14,716 --> 00:00:16,573
Here's the plan, in five parts.

7
00:00:16,813 --> 00:00:21,476
One, the contract. Two, a request, line by
line. Three, the server side.

8
00:00:21,716 --> 00:00:25,863
Four, who calls APIs. And five, the
mistakes that break them.

9
00:00:27,863 --> 00:00:33,426
API stands for Application Programming
Interface. Strip away the acronym, and

10
00:00:33,426 --> 00:00:34,582
it's a contract.

11
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:39,743
One program asks another program to do
something, without knowing how it gets

12
00:00:39,743 --> 00:00:40,510
done inside.

13
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:45,755
The handbook's glossary puts it the same
way: a defined contract, with no need

14
00:00:45,755 --> 00:00:47,488
to know the implementation.

15
00:00:47,938 --> 00:00:52,595
Think of a wall socket. You plug in a
lamp, and you know nothing about the power

16
00:00:52,595 --> 00:00:53,061
station.

17
00:00:53,301 --> 00:00:55,464
The shape of the socket is the promise.

18
00:00:55,914 --> 00:01:00,275
Or think of a restaurant. You read the
menu, you tell the waiter what you want,

19
00:01:00,275 --> 00:01:01,655
and the kitchen cooks it.

20
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:07,021
You never see the kitchen. It can swap
chefs, change the stove, even rewrite the

21
00:01:07,021 --> 00:01:07,469
recipe.

22
00:01:07,709 --> 00:01:11,135
As long as the dish matches the menu, you
never notice.

23
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:17,227
That's the whole value of an API. Both
sides can change independently, as long

24
00:01:17,227 --> 00:01:18,878
as the contract holds.

25
00:01:20,878 --> 00:01:27,560
This handbook focuses on web APIs. A
client sends an HTTP request, and a server

26
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,421
sends back a response.

27
00:01:29,661 --> 00:01:34,686
The client is anything that starts the
conversation. A browser, a phone app,

28
00:01:34,686 --> 00:01:36,405
another service, a script.

29
00:01:36,645 --> 00:01:42,182
MDN puts it plainly: the client always
initiates. The server never does.

30
00:01:42,632 --> 00:01:46,722
Here's a real request. GET /users/42.

31
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:51,711
GET is the method. It says what you want
to do. The path says which thing you

32
00:01:51,711 --> 00:01:52,019
want.

33
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:58,129
Headers add context. Accept says you want
JSON. Authorization says who you are.

34
00:01:58,579 --> 00:02:02,738
The server answers with a status code. 200
means OK.

35
00:02:02,978 --> 00:02:09,621
Status codes come in families. 200s mean
success. 4xx mean the caller made a

36
00:02:09,621 --> 00:02:12,505
mistake. 5xx mean the server did.

37
00:02:12,745 --> 00:02:16,803
Then comes the body, in JSON. An id, a
name, an email.

38
00:02:17,043 --> 00:02:22,886
Nothing in this response says whether the
data came from PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or

39
00:02:22,886 --> 00:02:25,588
a plain dictionary. That's the point.

40
00:02:26,038 --> 00:02:31,882
One more thing from MDN. HTTP is
stateless. Every request stands alone, so the

41
00:02:31,882 --> 00:02:37,726
server doesn't remember the last one
unless you send something that identifies

42
00:02:37,726 --> 00:02:38,026
you.

43
00:02:40,026 --> 00:02:45,711
Now the other side. This is a complete API
endpoint, written in FastAPI, the

44
00:02:45,711 --> 00:02:48,404
framework this handbook builds with.

45
00:02:48,644 --> 00:02:54,183
One decorator maps GET /users/{user_id} to
a Python function.

46
00:02:54,423 --> 00:03:00,129
The function looks the user up. If there's
no such user, it raises a 404.

47
00:03:00,369 --> 00:03:06,495
And because user id is typed as an
integer, FastAPI rejects anything else before

48
00:03:06,495 --> 00:03:08,027
your code even runs.

49
00:03:08,477 --> 00:03:10,682
Run it, and ask for user 42.

50
00:03:10,922 --> 00:03:13,946
You get the status line, the headers, and
the JSON.

51
00:03:14,186 --> 00:03:20,506
Ask for a user that doesn't exist, and you
get a 404. Ask for the word banana,

52
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:21,964
and you get a 422.

53
00:03:22,414 --> 00:03:27,946
Today that dictionary is fake. Next week
it could be PostgreSQL, or a call to

54
00:03:27,946 --> 00:03:29,095
another service.

55
00:03:29,335 --> 00:03:35,521
Not one line the client depends on
changes. Same URL, same JSON shape, same

56
00:03:35,521 --> 00:03:36,593
status codes.

57
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:38,902
That is the contract doing its job.

58
00:03:40,902 --> 00:03:43,361
Who calls APIs? More than you think.

59
00:03:43,601 --> 00:03:48,319
Your weather app. The sign in with Google
button. A payment form. Another

60
00:03:48,319 --> 00:03:49,353
backend service.

61
00:03:49,593 --> 00:03:56,130
And now, AI. Every call to a language
model through code is a web API: a POST

62
00:03:56,130 --> 00:04:00,120
request with a JSON body, and JSON coming
back.

63
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:06,019
Same contract. The kitchen is just a very
large model you will never see inside.

64
00:04:06,259 --> 00:04:11,886
Postman's twenty twenty-five survey of
over fifty-seven hundred developers found

65
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:14,911
that eighty-nine percent use generative
AI.

66
00:04:15,151 --> 00:04:20,388
But only twenty-four percent design their
APIs with AI agents in mind.

67
00:04:20,628 --> 00:04:25,900
The same survey found sixty-five percent
of organizations earn revenue from

68
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,361
their APIs. Contracts are products.

69
00:04:30,361 --> 00:04:36,881
Mistake one. Saying API when you mean REST
API. APIs also include function

70
00:04:36,881 --> 00:04:43,753
signatures and operating system calls.
REST is one architectural style for web

71
00:04:43,753 --> 00:04:44,194
APIs.

72
00:04:44,644 --> 00:04:50,110
Mistake two. Copying your database columns
straight into your responses. Rename

73
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:55,299
a column, and every client breaks. Design
the contract first, then the code

74
00:04:55,299 --> 00:04:55,991
behind it.

75
00:04:56,441 --> 00:05:02,342
Mistake three. Returning 200 OK for
everything, even errors. The status code is

76
00:05:02,342 --> 00:05:04,508
part of the contract. Use it.

77
00:05:04,958 --> 00:05:10,768
Mistake four. Confusing the API with the
whole system, or with the user

78
00:05:10,768 --> 00:05:17,151
interface. A UI consumes an API. One API
can serve a website and a mobile app.

79
00:05:17,601 --> 00:05:23,521
And mistake five. Leaving the contract
undocumented. If callers have to reverse

80
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:29,367
engineer your behavior, it's not a
contract. Part three of the handbook covers

81
00:05:29,367 --> 00:05:31,240
OpenAPI for exactly this.

82
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:34,208
Let's recap.

83
00:05:34,448 --> 00:05:37,759
An API is a contract between two programs.

84
00:05:37,999 --> 00:05:42,196
A web API is an HTTP request and a
response.

85
00:05:42,436 --> 00:05:44,602
The server behind it is free to change.

86
00:05:44,842 --> 00:05:47,901
Anyone can be the caller, even an AI
model.

87
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:50,597
And your job is to protect the contract.

88
00:05:51,047 --> 00:05:55,125
Next up, Part one of the handbook: how the
internet actually works.

89
00:05:55,365 --> 00:06:00,989
Before you go, try the chapter's exercise:
add a GET /users endpoint, and decide

90
00:06:00,989 --> 00:06:04,434
what it should return when the database is
empty.

91
00:06:04,674 --> 00:06:07,010
Subscribe, and I'll see you in the next
one.

